                        "entry_id": "45f1381b-8bda-33a0-95e7-1524df48af13",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A maund; a basket or hamper."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Scotland",
                            "dialectal"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "30decb44-732e-4961-a82f-79c18f498d7a",
                                "sense_id": "c9000e97-c9a4-30ce-9490-ddaf0a6e7275",
                                "text": "An apple-mill and press had been erected on the spot, to which some men were bringing fruit from divers points in mawn-baskets, while others were grinding them, and others wringing down the pomace, whose sweet juice gushed forth into tubs and pails."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e73b249f-a85b-30b5-b216-9fa62059b3fc",
                        "entry_id": "45f1381b-8bda-33a0-95e7-1524df48af13",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A ghost."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ecf5b519-47d0-41ec-982b-d318138367ce",
                                "sense_id": "e73b249f-a85b-30b5-b216-9fa62059b3fc",
                                "text": "None of the natives who had come in the boat would touch the body, or even go near it, saying, the mawn would come; that is literally, ‘the spirit of the deceased would seize them’."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "ffb78bdb-e389-323a-9ef5-33242ba450a6",
                "word": "mawn",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6e89dddb-3b62-3e84-9cc8-3cf59d918cf9",
                        "entry_id": "ffb78bdb-e389-323a-9ef5-33242ba450a6",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Peat."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "b1578158-2cec-408c-9a20-26922608c658",
                                "sense_id": "6e89dddb-3b62-3e84-9cc8-3cf59d918cf9",
                                "text": "[…] there lived, high up the breast of one of the loftiest mountains, in a hut among the black mawn-pits, - the world of human haunts in soundless depth below, above her only the cloud, the crag, the kite, - a melancholy woman[…]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ffaa16c2-6065-4546-abb0-9f6ebaf67165",
                                "sense_id": "6e89dddb-3b62-3e84-9cc8-3cf59d918cf9",
                                "text": "[…] mawn pits on a distant part of the Common. It is a bad mawn harvest this year in consequence of the wet summer and what with the dear coal and bread and meat and the diseased potatoes, I don't know what the poor people will do."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "42af2600-d265-45de-8765-7d70c3b7bc68",
                                "sense_id": "6e89dddb-3b62-3e84-9cc8-3cf59d918cf9",
                                "text": "[…] the wastes made impassable by crevasses; the mawn-pits that could swallow a man and his mount before either was aware of them."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8151eb86-404e-41ec-b1fa-168748c88fe0",
                                "sense_id": "6e89dddb-3b62-3e84-9cc8-3cf59d918cf9",
                                "text": "There are some extensive mawn pits near this village, from whence the inhabitants procure their chief fuel: it is cut up and harvested in the summer, and with a little wood makes a very good fire."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3d5eca36-7699-47d6-adcb-aab4ef4eb655",
                                "sense_id": "6e89dddb-3b62-3e84-9cc8-3cf59d918cf9",
                                "text": "[…] throughout the mawn pools and larger bogs of Radnorshire but the largest numbers in Wales are found in the old county of […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
